markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Evaluating the Impact of Models

Your students have spent this section teaching a computer to sense the world, bits into images, features into sound. That's only half the story. 

In curriculum Lesson 11, students dive into evaluating the impact of models, judging them through the lenses of fairness and safety.

Once a model can sense and predict, a harder question shows up: is it making the right call?

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><b>&#128161; Teacher tip:</b> students will want to answer that question with a number, whichever model has the highest accuracy or the lowest error. Push back gently when that happens. The activities in this lesson are built so that the "best" model by the numbers is sometimes the wrong one to actually use.</div>
</div>

## Spot the Machine Learning

Before students evaluate models, they need to notice where models already are. 

**This warm up asks:** in each everyday scenario, is a machine learning model classifying something into categories, or predicting a number?

<div style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden; margin:20px 0;">
  <iframe
    src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/AIFY2/unit-2/spot-the-ml-sort.html"
    width="100%"
    height="780"
    frameborder="0"
    scrolling="no"
    title="Spot the Machine Learning: sort scenarios into classification or regression">
  </iframe>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <p>&#128161; <strong>Teacher tip:</strong> the basketball scenario is the trickiest one for students, both in this demo and with your class. If they're stuck, prompt them to think about what data a sports team already collects on its players, and what that data could be used to predict.</p>
</div>

## Lesson Vocabulary

Two terms frame how your students will judge every model in this lesson, click each card to see its definition:

<div style="width:100%; border-radius:10px; overflow:hidden; margin:20px 0;">
  <iframe
    src="https://amandadennard-dotcom.github.io/AIFY2/unit-2/fairness-safety-flip-cards.html"
    width="100%"
    height="280"
    frameborder="0"
    scrolling="no"
    title="Fairness and safety vocabulary flip cards">
  </iframe>
</div>

Up next, students put both of these to the test, comparing real classification and regression models to decide which one is actually making the right decision.

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 20px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <img src="https://images.code.org/5ec7d742efa4da97d9ae60b79611ef3d-image-1790625929941.png" alt="Microsoft Foundry logo" style="width: 24px; height: 24px; vertical-align: middle; margin-right: 6px;"> <strong>AI-901 connection:</strong> The exam asks students to describe fairness, and reliability and safety, considerations in an AI solution. Your students practice the same thinking when they judge whether a model is making the right call.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
